import { THEMES } from "./site-draft"
/**
 * The images a marketer drops into a Facebook group — MTC-FCS-037.
 * ============================================================================
 * Drawn on a canvas rather than shipped as flat files, because every one of
 * them needs a different QR code and a different headline. The layout is data;
 * the studio (src/components/ads/AdStudio.js) draws it.
 *
 * ── THE FORMAT, AND WHY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A group POST is not an ad, and the sizes differ:
 *
 *   1080 × 1350 (4:5)  the default. The tallest image Facebook shows in-feed
 *                      without cropping, so it takes the most screen on a
 *                      phone — which is where a group is read.
 *   1080 × 1080 (1:1)  the safe one. Square survives every surface Facebook
 *                      re-crops for: notifications, link previews, the mobile
 *                      "photos" grid, and the desktop feed's 500px column.
 *
 * Neither needs the old 20%-text rule. That was an ADVERTISING policy, it was
 * retired, and it never applied to an ordinary post in a group — which is why
 * these can carry a real headline and a list of what you get.
 *
 * ── THE SAFE AREA ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Facebook crops a 4:5 image to a square in some places. Everything that has
 * to be read — headline, price, the QR — sits inside the CENTRE square, so a
 * crop loses decoration and never the message.
 *
 * ── THE QR SLOT IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY ──────────────────────────────────────
 * Each template reserves a square with a dashed outline and prints nothing in
 * it. A fresh QR is generated per marketer and dropped in (MTC-FCS-040), so
 * the slot is part of the layout rather than something to squeeze in later.
 * It is never smaller than 240px at 1080 wide: a QR photographed off somebody
 * else's phone screen needs the modules to survive being resampled twice.
 */

